    <h2 id="intro">Everyone Wants the Tactic</h2>

    <p>I get some version of the same question almost every week: "What do we need to do for AEO?"</p>

    <p>Usually it comes right after the brand notices a competitor showing up in ChatGPT or Google's AI Overviews and they don't. And usually the person asking wants a list: add this file, restructure these pages, publish these listicles, get mentioned in these threads.</p>

    <p>I understand the instinct. I've run an ecommerce company for more than 13 years, and for most of that time the playbook was tactical. Find the lever, pull the lever, watch the number move.</p>

    <p>But when I look at the brands asking, the problem is rarely tactical. The homepage describes the product in language no customer uses. The category they want to own is covered by one thin page. The product can do the thing, but nowhere on the site does it say so clearly. And nothing outside their own website corroborates the claim.</p>

    <div class="warnbox">
      <p>That's not an AEO problem. <strong>It's a clarity problem that AI is exposing.</strong></p>
    </div>

    <p class="pull">If you can't clearly explain what you do, who it's for, and why someone should choose you, you have a bigger problem than whether ChatGPT mentions you.</p>

    
    <section class="chunk" id="tempting">
      <div class="sec-label"><i>1</i> The Habit <span></span></div>
      <h2>Why the Hack Mindset Is So Tempting</h2>

      <p>SEO trained a generation of marketers to think in algorithms. We studied ranking factor reports. We panicked over updates. We tracked keyword positions against page changes and reverse-engineered what moved them. Being an SEO expert meant knowing the levers.</p>

      <p>A lot of those levers break in an AI answer.</p>

      <div class="tablewrap">
        <table class="prep">
          <thead><tr><th>The SEO-era lever</th><th>Why it doesn't transfer cleanly to AI answers</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Title and CTR testing</td><td>There's often no click to test. The answer is the result.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Conversion testing on organic pages</td><td>Less organic traffic means less volume to test against.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Measuring a refresh by traffic lift</td><td>The AI summary can absorb the value without sending the visit.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>"They outrank us on backlinks"</td><td>An AI summary isn't a ranked list of pages. It's a synthesis.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Rank #1 and you win</td><td>You can rank first and still not be the brand the answer recommends.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>That last row frustrates people most. Ranking still matters, because Google's AI features pull from its core search systems. But ranking doesn't guarantee the recommendation. The answer is assembled from what the system understands about your category, your brand, and the evidence surrounding both.</p>
    </section>

    
    <section class="chunk" id="google">
      <div class="sec-label"><i>2</i> The Source <span></span></div>
      <h2>What Google Actually Says</h2>

      <p>It's worth reading Google's own guidance, because it's unusually direct.</p>

      <p>On whether AEO and GEO are something new: "From Google Search's perspective, optimizing for generative AI search is optimizing for the search experience, and thus still SEO." On the tactics being sold: "many suggested 'hacks' aren't effective or supported by how Google Search actually works" (<a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/ai-optimization-guide">Google Search Central</a>).</p>

      <div class="tablewrap">
        <table class="prep">
          <thead><tr><th>Popular AEO tactic</th><th>What Google says</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>llms.txt and "special" AI files</td><td>"You don't need to create new machine readable files, AI text files, markup, or Markdown," and Google Search ignores them (<a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/ai-optimization-guide">Google</a>)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Chunking content for AI</td><td>"There's no requirement to break your content into tiny pieces for AI to better understand it" (<a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/ai-optimization-guide">Google</a>)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Special schema for AI Overviews</td><td>"There's also no special schema.org structured data that you need to add" (<a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/ai-features">Google</a>)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Special requirements overall</td><td>"There are no additional requirements to appear in AI Overviews or AI Mode, nor other special optimizations necessary" (<a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/ai-features">Google</a>)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="takeaway">
        <span class="tag">The principle</span>
        <p>Google is one answer engine, not all of them. But the principle holds: these systems retrieve what exists, synthesize it, and cross-check it. There's no hidden file that changes what they believe about you.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    
    <section class="chunk" id="listicle">
      <div class="sec-label"><i>3</i> The Trap <span></span></div>
      <h2>The Self-Promotional Listicle Trap</h2>

      <p>One of the most common shortcuts right now is publishing "The 10 Best [Category] Brands for 2026" on your own site, and ranking yourself first.</p>

      <p>Think about how that looks from the outside. It's a restaurant hanging a banner that says "Voted Best in Town" when nobody else in town has ever voted. The banner isn't the problem. The lack of anyone agreeing with it is.</p>

      <p>AI systems are built to notice that. A claim that only exists on your own site, unsupported by reviews, credible coverage, community discussion, or independent comparisons, doesn't carry much weight. Category leaders rarely need these tactics because the rest of the web already makes the case for them.</p>

      <p>That doesn't make comparison content bad. Honest comparisons that explain real differences and real tradeoffs are some of the most useful content a brand can publish. The difference is whether you're helping someone decide or trying to manufacture a position you haven't earned.</p>

      <p class="pull">You can't claim a spot on the shortlist that the rest of the internet doesn't back up.</p>
    </section>

    
    <section class="chunk" id="alignment">
      <div class="sec-label"><i>4</i> The Diagnosis <span></span></div>
      <h2>The Real Problem Is Alignment</h2>

      <p>When a brand isn't showing up in AI answers, I start with one question: do the company's positioning, product capabilities, and public evidence actually line up?</p>

      
      <figure>
        <div class="fig-frame">
          <p class="fig-kicker">Figure 1</p>
          <p class="fig-title">AI recommends you where three things agree</p>
          <div class="scroller">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 760 430" role="img" aria-labelledby="f1t f1d">
              <title id="f1t">Three overlapping circles showing brand alignment</title>
              <desc id="f1d">Three circles: what you want to be known for, how you describe yourself, and what AI and the web say about you. The center where all three overlap is labeled where AI recommends you.</desc>
              <g fill-opacity="0.12" stroke-width="2">
                <circle cx="315" cy="150" r="108" fill="#1f6feb" stroke="#1f6feb"/>
                <circle cx="445" cy="150" r="108" fill="#1f6feb" stroke="#1f6feb"/>
                <circle cx="380" cy="258" r="108" fill="#1f6feb" stroke="#1f6feb"/>
              </g>
              <g stroke="#9aa2ae" stroke-width="1.5" fill="none">
                <line x1="185" y1="118" x2="232" y2="130"/>
                <line x1="575" y1="118" x2="528" y2="130"/>
                <line x1="380" y1="386" x2="380" y2="366"/>
              </g>
              <g font-family="-apple-system,Segoe UI,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif">
                <text x="178" y="104" text-anchor="end" font-size="16" font-weight="780" fill="#12161c">What you want</text>
                <text x="178" y="124" text-anchor="end" font-size="16" font-weight="780" fill="#12161c">to be known for</text>
                <text x="178" y="144" text-anchor="end" font-size="13" fill="#6b7480">category, use case, buyer</text>

                <text x="582" y="104" text-anchor="start" font-size="16" font-weight="780" fill="#12161c">How you</text>
                <text x="582" y="124" text-anchor="start" font-size="16" font-weight="780" fill="#12161c">describe yourself</text>
                <text x="582" y="144" text-anchor="start" font-size="13" fill="#6b7480">homepage, PDPs, FAQs</text>

                <text x="380" y="404" text-anchor="middle" font-size="16" font-weight="780" fill="#12161c">What AI and the web say about you</text>
                <text x="380" y="423" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#6b7480">AI answers, reviews, press, communities</text>
              </g>
              <rect x="312" y="164" width="136" height="50" rx="10" fill="#1f6feb"/>
              <g font-family="-apple-system,Segoe UI,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffffff">
                <text x="380" y="185" font-size="14" font-weight="780">Where AI</text>
                <text x="380" y="203" font-size="14" font-weight="780">recommends you</text>
              </g>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <figcaption>Most brands that struggle with AI visibility have two circles that barely touch. The work is pulling all three into the middle.</figcaption>
        </div>
      </figure>

      <p>When all three overlap, you have a real shot at the recommendation. When they don't, you get the most common failure I see: a brand that wants to be known for one thing, describes itself as something slightly different, and is described by AI as something else entirely.</p>

      <div class="tablewrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>What the brand wants</th><th>What the site says</th><th>What AI says</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>"The best seat cover for large dogs"</td><td>"Premium vehicle interior protection solutions"</td><td>"A general car accessories brand"</td></tr>
            <tr><td>"The go-to option for hot climates"</td><td>Material specs, no mention of heat</td><td>Recommends a competitor with a heat buying guide</td></tr>
            <tr><td>"Built for frequent travelers"</td><td>One lifestyle photo of a road trip</td><td>Doesn't mention the brand for travel prompts</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>None of those gaps is solved by a file, a chunking pass, or a listicle. Each one is solved by saying the right thing clearly, on the right page, and giving the web a reason to agree.</p>
    </section>

    
    <section class="chunk" id="vewo">
      <div class="sec-label"><i>5</i> The Method <span></span></div>
      <h2>How We Approach It at VEWO</h2>

      <p>This is exactly why VEWO runs on what we call the Operator Method: a repeating 90-day cycle. It isn't a one-time AEO project. It's an ongoing engine.</p>

      
      <figure>
        <div class="fig-frame">
          <p class="fig-kicker">Figure 2</p>
          <p class="fig-title">The Operator Method</p>
          <div class="os">
            <div class="os-track four">
              <div class="os-step"><div class="os-num">1</div><div><h4>Audit</h4><p>AI perception vs. positioning vs. site copy</p></div></div>
              <div class="os-step"><div class="os-num">2</div><div><h4>Plan</h4><p>Prioritize gaps by commercial value</p></div></div>
              <div class="os-step"><div class="os-num">3</div><div><h4>Execute</h4><p>Build on-site content and distribution strategy</p></div></div>
              <div class="os-step"><div class="os-num">4</div><div><h4>Evaluate</h4><p>Track buying prompts and business signals</p></div></div>
            </div>
            <div class="os-repeat">↻ &nbsp;Every 90 days, informed by what moved and what didn't</div>
          </div>
          <figcaption>An engine, not a campaign. Each cycle starts from what the last one learned.</figcaption>
        </div>
      </figure>

      <p><strong>Audit.</strong> We start with an AI perception audit, comparing what the brand wants to be known for, how it describes itself, and what answer engines actually say when asked the questions that matter. We pull the real queries driving impressions to key pages from Google Search Console and Bing Webmaster Tools and compare that language to the page copy. Then we layer in the questions customers ask in reviews, support tickets, and sales conversations.</p>

      <p><strong>Plan.</strong> We prioritize the gaps by commercial importance. Some need a new page. Some need a rewritten product or category page. Some need a comparison, an FAQ, or a buying guide. Sometimes the right answer is a conversation with the product team: can the product actually do this, and is it a strong enough use case to claim?</p>

      <p><strong>Execute.</strong> We research, write, build, and publish the on-site content directly: FAQs, category pages, comparisons, buying guides, and blogs. We also provide the structure and strategy for distribution — which platforms, what cadence, what messaging, and in what order. The client's team executes the posting, outreach, and placement, because off-site credibility has to be real.</p>

      <p><strong>Evaluate.</strong> We track a defined set of buying prompts, search visibility, and the business signals that matter. Then the next 90 days begin.</p>
    </section>

    
    <section class="chunk" id="wont">
      <div class="sec-label"><i>6</i> The Line <span></span></div>
      <h2>What We Won't Sell You</h2>

      <p>Because the market is full of tactical promises, it's worth being explicit about what VEWO doesn't do.</p>

      <ul class="loses">
        <li><b>llms.txt as a visibility strategy.</b> Google says Search ignores it.</li>
        <li><b>Content chunking.</b> Google says there's no requirement to break content into tiny pieces.</li>
        <li><b>Schema as a strategy.</b> Structured data is good hygiene. It isn't what earns a recommendation.</li>
        <li><b>Mention farming or manufactured Reddit threads.</b> Fake corroboration is still fake, and it tends to get noticed.</li>
        <li><b>Listicles ranking our clients first.</b> A claim nobody else supports isn't authority.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="takeaway">
        <span class="tag">What we do</span>
        <p>Find the questions buyers are asking, answer them clearly and credibly on the brand's own site, build the strategy to earn real mentions elsewhere, and repeat every 90 days.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    
    <section class="chunk" id="prompts">
      <div class="sec-label"><i>7</i> The Prompts <span></span></div>
      <h2>Build Prompts Around Real Buying Decisions</h2>

      <p>One of the fastest ways to waste time in AEO is tracking the wrong prompts. Broad definitional prompts like "what is a dog seat cover" or "best car accessories" are noisy, highly competitive, and rarely where a purchase gets decided.</p>

      <div class="srcs">
        <div class="src">
          <h4><span>&#10005;</span>Noisy prompts</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>Broad definitions</li>
            <li>Generic "best of" categories</li>
            <li>Dozens of redundant variations</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="src">
          <h4><span>&#10003;</span>Buying prompts</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>Selection for a specific need</li>
            <li>Comparisons against named alternatives</li>
            <li>"Alternatives to [competitor]" and specific requirements</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="flowset">
        <div class="flowrow now">
          <span class="rowlabel">An illustrative buying prompt</span>
          <div class="steps steps1"><em>"Which waterproof dog seat covers work with a split rear bench and still leave room for a child car seat?"</em></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>That's a real buying requirement. Build prompt sets from customer language and search data, separate branded from non-branded questions, and cut the redundant variations.</p>

      <p class="pull">The goal isn't appearing for every broad definition. It's belonging in the evaluation when a real buyer is deciding.</p>
    </section>

    
    <section class="chunk" id="measure">
      <div class="sec-label"><i>8</i> The Scorecard <span></span></div>
      <h2>Measure What Actually Matters</h2>

      <p>A rising brand mention count feels good. It isn't the goal. Buying journeys rarely follow a straight line anymore, and the last click won't tell you an AI answer started it.</p>

      
      <figure>
        <div class="fig-frame">
          <p class="fig-kicker">Figure 3</p>
          <p class="fig-title">One purchase, four touchpoints, one visible click</p>
          <div class="flowset">
            <div class="flowrow now">
              <span class="rowlabel">A typical journey</span>
              <div class="steps steps4">
                <em>Discovers the brand in an AI answer</em><em>Researches it on Google</em><em>Reads reviews and comparisons</em><em>Buys later via branded search</em>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <figcaption>Last-click attribution credits the branded search. The AI answer that started it shows up nowhere. That's why you triangulate instead.</figcaption>
        </div>
      </figure>

      <div class="grid6">
        <div class="g6"><b>Prompt presence</b><span>In the buying prompts that matter, not every prompt</span></div>
        <div class="g6"><b>Description accuracy</b><span>How AI describes you vs. how you want to be described</span></div>
        <div class="g6"><b>Branded search</b><span>Often picks up what AI discovery starts</span></div>
        <div class="g6"><b>Qualified visits</b><span>To the pages built to close the gap</span></div>
        <div class="g6"><b>Conversion</b><span>From the categories you're trying to own</span></div>
        <div class="g6"><b>Revenue</b><span>The only number that settles the argument</span></div>
      </div>

      <div class="warnbox">
        <p>If mentions go up and none of those move, <strong>you haven't earned anything yet.</strong></p>
      </div>
    </section>

    
    <section class="chunk" id="faq">
      <div class="sec-label"><i>9</i> Questions <span></span></div>
      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="faq">
        <details open>
          <summary>Is AEO different from SEO?</summary>
          <div class="answer"><p>Google's position is that optimizing for generative AI search "is optimizing for the search experience, and thus still SEO." The fundamentals carry over. What changes is that the recommendation depends more on how clearly and credibly your brand is understood, not just where a page ranks.</p></div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Do we need an llms.txt file?</summary>
          <div class="answer"><p>Not for Google. Google says Search ignores llms.txt and similar files, and that creating one will neither help nor hurt your visibility there. It's fine to maintain one for other systems, but it isn't a visibility strategy.</p></div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Should we publish "best of" listicles that include our brand?</summary>
          <div class="answer"><p>Only if the claim is genuinely supported elsewhere. Self-ranked listicles without third-party corroboration rarely carry weight. Honest comparisons that explain real tradeoffs are far more useful to buyers and to AI systems.</p></div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What's an AI perception audit?</summary>
          <div class="answer"><p>A structured comparison of three things: what your brand wants to be known for, how your site actually describes you, and what AI answer engines say when asked real buying questions. The gaps between them are your roadmap.</p></div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How long does it take to see results?</summary>
          <div class="answer"><p>Content and clarity changes can influence AI answers within weeks as pages are recrawled. Earning consistent inclusion in competitive buying prompts usually takes multiple 90-day cycles, because the off-site corroboration has to build too.</p></div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>

    
    <section class="chunk" id="conclusion">
      <div class="sec-label"><i>10</i> Conclusion <span></span></div>
      <h2>Earn It</h2>

      <p>There's no AEO algorithm to hack. There's no file that makes ChatGPT recommend you, no chunking pattern that rewrites what Google believes, and no listicle that manufactures authority you don't have.</p>

      <p>What there is, is work. Clarify what you want to be known for. Make sure your site says it plainly, in the language customers actually use. Make sure the product can back it up. Give the rest of the web real reasons to agree. Track whether you belong in the buying conversations that matter. Then do it again next quarter.</p>

      <p>It's slower than a hack. It's also the only thing that lasts.</p>

      <p class="pull">You can't hack your way into an AI answer. You have to earn your way in.</p>
    </section>

